            <ArticleTitle>Effect of yogic intervention on the autonomic nervous system in the patients with coronary artery disease: a randomized controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>452-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this study 258 patients from the Department of Cardiology in Chhatrapati Shahuji Maharaj Medical University, Lucknow were selected to participate. All had been diagnosed with coronary artery disease. They were randomly divided into 2 groups, the yoga group and the non-yoga group, 129 in each group; 208 completed the study protocol. The yogic intervention consisted of 35-40 min/day, 5 days a week over a period of 18 months in the Department of Physiology. Autonomic function testing was done in both the groups at zero time and after 18 months. We observed a statistically significant reduction in body mass index, waist circumference, systolic and diastolic blood pressure, and heart rate (P &lt; 0.05), i.e., a significant positive effect was observed when yoga therapy was used as an adjunct in patients with coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
